 "source_type": "instagram",
 "source_url": "https://www.instagram.com/reel/Df6-tQ-Jch0/",
 "title": "When Someone CCs Your Boss to Pressure You",
 "topic": "communication",
 "summary": "The creator explains that when a colleague CCs your boss on an email to pressure you, you should avoid over-explaining or getting defensive. Instead, reply calmly by stating that the task is already on your priority list and asking if the manager wants to reprioritize given any urgency.",
 "key_points": [
  "When a colleague copies your boss, it is often to make you respond faster, not necessarily because the issue is serious.",
  "The mistake people make is immediately explaining why work is unfinished or why they couldn't respond earlier, which shifts the conversation into self-defense.",
  "Instead of defending yourself, reply calmly using the exact line: \"Thanks for flagging this. This is already on my priority list and I'm working towards the agreed timeline. Happy to reprioritize if there's any change in the urgency.\"",
  "Using this response avoids long explanations, unnecessary apologies, and a defensive tone.",
  "This approach shows your manager that you are aware of your responsibilities, have a plan, and understand that changing priorities requires a management decision."
 ],
 "content": "When Someone CCs Your Boss to Pressure You. You know that one colleague who suddenly copies your boss on an email not because the issue is serious, but because they want you to respond faster. Welcome to episode number 12, do this at work. And the moment you see your manager in CC, what you do? You start explaining yourself why the work isn't finished, why you have been doing, why you couldn't respond earlier. That's exactly where you make that mistake. Because now, instead of discussing the work, you're defending yourself. Here is what I would do instead. Reply calmly. Thanks for flagging this. This is already on my priority list and I'm working towards the agreed timeline. Happy to reprioritize if there's any change in the urgency. That's it. No long explanation, no unnecessary apology, no defensive tone. And here is what you have just done. You have shown your manager that you are aware of your responsibilities, you have communicated that there is already a plan and you have made it clear that changing priorities requires a decision. Because sometimes the most powerful response in corporate isn't a clever comeback, it's refusing to be pulled into an unnecessary reaction. Share this with a friend who needs to hear this and follow for more. Text shown on screen: DO THIS AT WORK #12, When Some CCs your boss to pressure you.",
 "status": "done",
